{0 Owebview}

A thin OCaml binding to {{:https://github.com/webview/webview}webview}: open a
native window backed by the operating system's web engine, load HTML/CSS/JS,
and bridge JavaScript with OCaml — no Electron, no bundler.

{1 Quickstart}

A whole application is about ten lines:

{[
  let () =
    let w = Webview.create () in
    Webview.set_title w "My first owebview app";
    Webview.set_size w ~width:480 ~height:320 Webview.Hint_none;
    Webview.set_html w "<h1>Hello from OCaml</h1>";
    Webview.run w;
    Webview.destroy w
]}

Call OCaml from the page and answer the JavaScript promise:

{[
  Webview.bind w "add" (fun id req ->
      let result =
        match Scanf.sscanf_opt req "[%d,%d]" (fun a b -> a + b) with
        | Some n -> string_of_int n
        | None -> "null"
      in
      Webview.return w id ~error:false ~result)
]}

{1 API}

The whole binding is the {!module:Webview} module. A tour of it:

- {b Lifecycle}: {!Webview.create}, {!Webview.run}, {!Webview.terminate},
  {!Webview.destroy}
- {b Content}: {!Webview.navigate}, {!Webview.set_html}, {!Webview.eval}
- {b JavaScript bridge}: {!Webview.bind}, {!Webview.unbind}, {!Webview.return}
- {b Threading}: {!Webview.dispatch}
- {b Native handles}: {!Webview.get_native_handle}
- {b Desktop integration} (library [owebview.desktop]):
  {!Webview_desktop.Icon.set_app_icon}, {!Webview_desktop.Icon.set_app_id},
  {!Webview_desktop.Dialog.confirm}, {!Webview_desktop.Dialog.open_file}
- {b Locating on-disk assets}: {!Webview.Utils.web_dir}

{1 Native dependencies}

webview uses the system web engine, so its native libraries are needed:

- {b macOS}: WebKit / Cocoa, provided by the system.
- {b Linux}: [gtk+-3.0] and [webkit2gtk-4.1] ([-dev] packages).
- {b Windows}: the WebView2 runtime (ships with Windows 10/11).

The platform-specific compile and link flags are detected automatically at
build time, so there is nothing to tweak by hand.
